  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{9\\cdot {3}^{2x}}{2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{3}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{3}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={3}^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{3}^{2x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070214/assets/052e461c4f02a644254b4b9936b688c29afacd07432db7234bd71474dc9a076c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
